Notebook[{
Cell["PadicN", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"70208e3f-64b8-452e-83be-da28710e393d"],

Cell["\<\
PadicN[r,p] retains an exact integer or rational source in a public \
PadicLazy[expr,p] representation. Arithmetic and Sqrt build refinable \
recipes; Normal certifies rational results or returns \
Missing[\"NotRational\"] for irrational root expressions. PadicN[r,p,n] \
eagerly returns PadicRationalN with positive relative precision n. \
PadicN[r,p,Automatic] is eager and selects minimum symmetric reconstruction \
precision for a rational source. PadicN[lazy,p,n] requests a snapshot; \
Automatic uses sufficient candidate bounds, not necessarily minimum \
precision. Existing finite approximations remain finite and cannot regain \
discarded digits. Sqrt of a finite snapshot preserves relative precision for \
odd primes and loses one bit at p=2; insufficient domain information gives \
Missing[\"InsufficientPrecision\"]. Bases must be prime. The constructor \
threads over lists. Display previews preserve sources.\
\>", "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"60368cb2-11ec-4ecc-b6dc-02d45f0d74ee"],

Cell["\<\
Padic[x,p] remains the exact periodic constructor. Two-argument PadicN is \
lazy; every three-argument PadicN call requests an eager finite snapshot.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"f2f84217-2aa8-4585-ab2b-24670d27906a"],

Cell["\<\
Lazy arithmetic retains rational sources and operation recipes. Normal uses \
propagated numerator and denominator bounds, modular arithmetic, and \
certified reconstruction. It does not normalize intermediate finite results.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"88975b24-652c-4fdd-aa83-0ff08ea04fed"],

Cell["\<\
PadicN[r,p,Automatic] selects the least positive n with p^n > 2 \
Max[Abs[u],v]^2 for the reduced normalized source r=p^e u/v. For a lazy \
recipe the bound can be conservative. Exact zero uses n=1 automatically.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"52bb46b8-aa76-4e84-9950-3bf336a503ac"],

Cell["\<\
Printing retains the lazy recipe through InterpretationBox. A source preview \
uses at most ten digits or its smaller reconstruction precision; an \
arithmetic preview uses ten relative digits. Display precision does not limit \
later evaluation.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"6770512e-110a-4f22-8e08-d2753fa6e804"],

Cell["\<\
Finite PadicRationalN inputs remain finite. Increasing requested precision \
cannot restore lost digits. Their Normal method retains its \
bounded-candidate, residue-fallback, and uncertain-zero behavior.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"268a7bf1-38a0-4c58-986a-142156eac858"],

Cell["\<\
Arithmetic between lazy rational sources can select the smaller prime base; \
recipes containing square roots require matching bases. Any finite operand \
requires a matching base and limits the result precision.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"586ebe1f-c83c-4783-827c-fab6006910d6"],

Cell["\<\
Lazy valuation, normalization, digits, and digit-tree rays request modular \
information. Zero powers and inverses are checked when the recipe is \
evaluated. There is no persistent cache or general digit-generator API.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"79097e59-7b65-4a5c-9946-3acc89969e68"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<PadicNumbers`\>\"", 
  "]"}]], "ExampleInitialization",ExpressionUUID->"7348192a-625b-429c-aac0-\
c2f71196e1f6"],

